<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Oretani</h1><p class="st-meta">500 BCE – 197 BCE (approx.) · capital Castulo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Iberian people of the upper Guadalquivir whose city Castulo controlled the silver mines of the Sierra Morena and gave Hannibal his wife.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Oretani lived in the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadalquivir">Guadalquivir</a> valley and the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meseta-central">Meseta</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciudad-real">Ciudad Real</a>, from the mid-first millennium BCE. They took their name from the town of Oretum, but their leading city was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castulo">Castulo</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linares">Linares</a>, a crossroads between the coast and the interior.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Their wealth came from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and lead mines of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-morena">Sierra Morena</a>, exploited intensively under Carthaginian and then Roman control. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castulo">Castulo</a> had rich princely tombs and later minted bronze coins with an Iberian legend. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himilce">Himilce</a>, said to be a princess of Castulo, tying the Oretani to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barcid-iberia">Barcid Iberia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 206 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castulo">Castulo</a> went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> during the Scipionic campaigns. The Oretani joined the revolt of 197 BCE but were absorbed into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hispania-tarraconensis">Tarraconensis</a>, and Castulo remained an important Roman mining town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/oretani.jpg" alt="Oretani" width="320" height="324"><figcaption>Image: Ángel M. Felicísimo · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moneda_ib%C3%A9rica_(19608039604).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 BCE – 197 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Castulo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Iberian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Iberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oretanians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=oretani" aria-label="Oretani on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4590 811 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Oretani lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4910 1067L4975 1081M4910 1067L5031 967"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4975 1081h0M5031 967h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4910 1067h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4910 1067h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Castulo. <a href="/atlas?year=-348&amp;focus=oretani">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
